# {{ project_name }}

A Django 6.1 project on PostgreSQL 18, with Redis 8 as its cache, made from the
django-starter template.

## First-time setup

You need [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and Docker.

```bash
git init                          # a new project isn't a git repo yet
cp .env.example .env              # sets DJANGO_ENV=development
uv sync                           # installs Python 3.14 and the packages, creates uv.lock
uv run pre-commit install         # turns on the checks that run on every commit (see Checks)
git add -A && git commit -m "Start from django-starter"   # the first commit (slow once: see Checks)
docker compose up -d              # starts Postgres and Redis in Docker
uv run python manage.py migrate
uv run python manage.py createsuperuser
uv run python manage.py runserver
```

Then open http://localhost:8000/admin/ and log in.

The first commit includes `uv.lock`: it pins every package version so everyone, and
the production image, installs exactly the same thing. Commit it whenever it changes.

## Everyday commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `docker compose up -d` / `docker compose stop` | Start / stop Postgres and Redis |
| `uv run python manage.py runserver` | Development server with auto-reload |
| `uv run python manage.py makemigrations` / `migrate` | Create / apply database changes |
| `uv run pytest` | Run the tests |
| `uv run pytest --cov` | Run the tests and measure coverage (fails below 80%) |
| `uv run pre-commit run --all-files` | Run the commit checks (ruff) on every file |
| pip-audit | Check the packages for known vulnerabilities: see [Before every pull request](#before-every-pull-request) |
| `uv run ruff check .` / `uv run ruff format .` | Check for problems / format the code |
| `docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml run --rm worker python manage.py prune_db_task_results` | Delete finished background jobs older than 14 days (production): see [Background jobs](#background-jobs) |
| `uv run python manage.py axes_reset` | Unlock everyone locked out by login rate limiting: see [Security](#security) |
| `uv add <package>` | Add a dependency (updates pyproject.toml and uv.lock) |
| `docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml -f compose.local-http.yaml up --build` | Build and run production on this machine (migrate step, then the app and the job worker) at http://localhost:8000/staff/: see [Production](#production-the-real-image-run-locally) |
| `copier update` | Pull in improvements from the starter template |

## Environments

`DJANGO_ENV` picks the environment. The settings are in `config/settings/`, one file
per topic, shared by every environment, and any individual setting can still be
overridden by its own environment variable.

Add a new setting to the file for its topic; a setting may be set in only one file (a
test checks this). Where a setting differs in tests, it's changed next to the setting
itself. For a new topic, create `config/settings/<topic>.py`, add it to the import and
the list in `config/settings/__init__.py`, and to `TOPICS` in
`tests/test_settings_package.py` (a test fails until both are done).

| `DJANGO_ENV` | Set by | Defaults |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `development` | `.env` on your machine | Debug on, plain HTTP, local secret key, `admin/` |
| `test` | pytest, automatically (pytest-env) | Debug off, plain HTTP, fast password hashing, in-memory cache (no Redis needed) |
| `production` | Nothing: it's what unset means (with Docker Compose, the values come from `.env.production`) | HTTPS-only with HSTS, debug forbidden, required values below |

Production refuses to start if `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`, `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS`,
`DJANGO_ADMIN_URL`, `DATABASE_URL` or `CACHE_URL` is missing, if the admin URL is `admin/`,
or if `DJANGO_DEBUG` is true. If `EMAIL_URL` is set, it must be `smtp+tls://` or
`smtp+ssl://` with a server address, and needs `DJANGO_DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL`. A misspelled `DJANGO_ENV` is refused everywhere.
`DATABASE_URL` and `CACHE_URL` are needed in development too; `.env.example` sets both.
`.env.example` lists and explains every variable.

## Running each environment

### Development

Start:

```bash
docker compose up -d                  # start Postgres and Redis in the background
uv run python manage.py runserver     # start Django at http://localhost:8000/admin/
```

`docker compose` loads `compose.yaml` plus `compose.override.yaml` (the development
settings: the dev login `app`/`app` for both, and Postgres on port 5432 and Redis on
port 6379, both open to this machine only).

**Redis** is Django's cache, shared by every server process. It saves nothing to disk
and holds at most 256 MB (change `--maxmemory` in `compose.yaml`); when full it
drops the keys used least recently. So treat anything in it as something that can
disappear: stopping or restarting Redis empties it. A request gives up on Redis after
2 seconds instead of hanging, and Django's Redis cache raises an error rather than
skipping it, so code that must keep working while Redis is down should catch cache
errors where it uses the cache.

**Port 5432 already in use** (for example by a Postgres installed on your machine):
`docker compose up -d` fails with an error about port 5432 ("address already in
use" or "port is already allocated"). Pick another port: in `.env`, add the first line
and change your `DATABASE_URL` line to the second, then start it again:

```dotenv
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5433/{{ project_slug }}
```

**Port 6379 already in use** (for example by a Redis installed on your machine): the
same error appears about port 6379. In `.env`, add the first line and change your
`CACHE_URL` line to the second:

```dotenv
REDIS_PORT=6380
CACHE_URL=redis://app:app@localhost:6380/0
```

Stop:

```bash
# Ctrl+C in the runserver terminal stops Django, then:
docker compose stop                   # stop both (the database is kept; the cache is emptied)
```

Check it: <http://localhost:8000/nope> should show Django's yellow debug page, and

```bash
uv run python manage.py shell -c "from django.conf import settings as s; print(s.DJANGO_ENV, s.DEBUG)"
# development True
```

### Test

Start:

```bash
docker compose up -d db               # the tests need only Postgres: they cache in memory, not Redis
uv run pytest                         # sets DJANGO_ENV=test itself
```

pytest creates and deletes its own throwaway database, so when it finishes
there is nothing left running except Postgres.

Stop:

```bash
docker compose stop                   # stop Postgres
```

### Production (the real image, run locally)

`compose.prod.yaml` runs production: Postgres and Redis, then a `migrate` step that applies
migrations and exits, then the app and the background-job `worker` (both only start
if `migrate` succeeded), and Caddy, the reverse proxy in front of the app. It's a
separate Compose project (`{{ project_slug }}-production`) with its own database, so it
never touches your development data.

Caddy (`deploy/Caddyfile`) is the only part the internet reaches, on ports 80 and 443:

- **HTTPS:** it gets and renews the certificate for the site's domain itself, from
  Let's Encrypt, and redirects plain HTTP to HTTPS. Certificates are kept in the
  `caddy_data` volume.
- **The app:** it forwards requests to Gunicorn, taking in each request and response in
  full first, so slow visitors tie up Caddy (which handles many at a time), not one of
  Gunicorn's few workers. It sets `X-Forwarded-Proto` and `X-Forwarded-For`, and the
  app always runs with `DJANGO_BEHIND_PROXY=true` (set in `compose.prod.yaml`).
- **Uploaded files:** it serves them from the `media` volume, always as downloads.
- **When the app doesn't answer** (a restart, or it's down): `templates/proxy_error.html`
  with the real status (502, 503 or 504).
- **`/healthz` and `/readyz`:** a 404 from outside (see the health checks below).

The app and the worker run locked down: no Linux capabilities, a read-only filesystem
(uploads go to the `media` volume). Redis runs the same way, as its own non-root user
with a login, and Caddy too, apart from being allowed to listen on ports 80 and 443.
The database and Redis are only on an internal network with no published port, and the
app has no published port: everything reaches it through Caddy.

Settings and secrets come from four files next to it, which git and the image all ignore:

| File | Read by | Holds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `.env.production` | the app, the worker and `migrate` | The production settings you need from `.env.example`: at least `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`, `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `DJANGO_ADMIN_URL`, `DATABASE_URL` and `CACHE_URL` (`redis://app:PASSWORD@redis:6379/0`). If the app sends email (password resets included), also `EMAIL_URL` and `DJANGO_DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL`: see [Email](#email). Never copy `.env.example` itself: it sets `DJANGO_ENV=development`. Leave `DJANGO_ENV` and `DJANGO_DEBUG` out (unset means production). |
| `.env.postgres` | Postgres | The database login: `POSTGRES_USER`, `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, `POSTGRES_DB`. The password must match the one in `DATABASE_URL`. |
| `.env.redis` | Redis | `REDIS_PASSWORD`, the password of Redis's `app` login. It must match the one in `CACHE_URL`. |
| `.env.caddy` | Caddy | `SITE_ADDRESS`, the site's domain (e.g. `example.com`, or `example.com, www.example.com` for both), which Caddy gets the certificate for. Not a secret. |

**To try it on your machine,** make the files once. This makes random passwords and
a random secret key; the files are created readable only by you:

```bash
(
  umask 077                                    # new files: only you can read them
  PASSWORD=$(python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))")
  SECRET_KEY=$(python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(50))")
  REDIS_PASSWORD=$(python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))")
  echo "REDIS_PASSWORD=$REDIS_PASSWORD" > .env.redis
  echo "SITE_ADDRESS=localhost" > .env.caddy   # compose.local-http.yaml uses plain HTTP instead
  cat > .env.postgres <<EOF
POSTGRES_USER=app
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=$PASSWORD
POSTGRES_DB={{ project_slug }}
EOF
  cat > .env.production <<EOF
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=$SECRET_KEY
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1
DJANGO_ADMIN_URL=staff/
DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:$PASSWORD@db:5432/{{ project_slug }}
CACHE_URL=redis://app:$REDIS_PASSWORD@redis:6379/0
EOF
)
```

Postgres only uses that password when it first creates the production database. If
you make the files again later, delete that database first (all its data is lost):
`docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml down -v`.

Stop `runserver` first, because both use port 8000. On your machine the site runs over
plain HTTP, so `compose.local-http.yaml` switches the HTTPS settings off and has Caddy
answer on <http://localhost:8000> without a certificate. It's only for this local
check, and never goes on a server:

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml -f compose.local-http.yaml up --build   # Ctrl+C stops it
# or in the background (it keeps running, even after a restart, until you stop it):
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml -f compose.local-http.yaml up --build -d --wait
```

With it running (in another terminal, or started with `-d`), make an admin login
(production has its own database), then open <http://localhost:8000/staff/>:

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml exec web python manage.py createsuperuser
```

If the app or the worker doesn't start, the `migrate` step probably failed. See why with:

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs migrate web worker
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs caddy    # the proxy: certificates, config errors
```

Stop:

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml down      # remove the containers (data is kept)
```

**On a real server** the same four files hold the real values: the site's domain in
`SITE_ADDRESS` and `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS`. Before the first start, point the domain's
DNS at the server and open ports 80 and 443 (Caddy needs both for the certificate).
Start it without `compose.local-http.yaml`, so HTTPS and its protections are on:
`docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml up --build -d --wait`, then check
`docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs caddy` for "certificate
obtained successfully".

The app trusts the proxy's headers because exactly one proxy, Caddy, sits in front of
it. With another proxy or a CDN in front of Caddy, the visitor's address is no longer
the last `X-Forwarded-For` entry: change `config/login_limits.py`, and Caddy's
`trusted_proxies`.

Each run of that command migrates first, while the old app and worker keep running, so a
migration must still work with the code already running (add things first, remove
them in a later release). There is no rollback to a previous image yet; a later
version of the starter adds versioned images and deploy steps.

Two health checks on the app answer whatever the Host header is, before the HTTPS
redirect, so something calling the app directly (by IP, often over plain HTTP) can poll
them. Caddy answers both with a 404 from outside: anyone could otherwise see when the
database is down, and each call costs a database and Redis query.

| Path | Answers `ok` when | Otherwise | Used by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `/healthz` | The app is running. It never touches the database or Redis | No answer | Docker's `HEALTHCHECK`. A database outage doesn't mark every app container unhealthy (which would fail `up --wait`, and make tools that restart unhealthy containers restart them all) |
| `/readyz` | The database and Redis answer too | 503 `not ready`, and a `Not ready: database failed` (or `cache`) warning in the logs | A load balancer in front of several app servers, calling each one directly, to stop sending visitors to one that can't serve them |

To call them yourself on the server:

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml exec web python -c "import urllib.request; print(urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/readyz').read())"
```

`/readyz` is optional: with one app server there's nowhere else to send visitors, so
leave it unused (Caddy doesn't health-check the app: a proxy that checks its only
server on `/readyz` would refuse every request during a short Redis blip). It checks
the database over a new connection each time, which gives up after 5 seconds
(`connect_timeout` in `config/settings/database.py`; a `?connect_timeout=10` in
`DATABASE_URL` overrides it), and Redis gives up after 2: measured with each one
frozen, `/readyz` answers 503 in 5 and 2 seconds.

The `worker` has its own Docker health check, `python -m config.health worker`, every
minute. It's unhealthy when the database doesn't answer, or when a job has waited more
than 5 minutes while no job has started in that time (`MAX_JOB_WAIT` in
`config/health.py`): the worker is stuck or stopped. A busy worker catching up on a
backlog keeps starting jobs, so it stays healthy. The first check runs a minute after
the worker starts, so `up --wait` takes about a minute longer than before.

When it's unhealthy, `docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml ps` shows it,
and `up --wait` fails. Docker doesn't restart it by itself. The check's own messages are
kept by Docker, not in the worker's logs:

```bash
docker inspect --format '{{ '{{' }}json .State.Health.Log{{ '}}' }}' {{ project_slug }}-production-worker-1   # why the check failed
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs worker      # what the worker was doing
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml restart worker   # then restart it
```

**Gunicorn**, the production server, takes its settings from `gunicorn.conf.py`:

- **Gunicorn workers (web requests, not background jobs):** set `WEB_CONCURRENCY` in the app's environment, about 2 x CPU cores + 1
  (default 1): in `.env.production` with Docker Compose, or `Environment=` in a
  systemd service. Each worker checking a login at the same moment uses about 100 MB
  (Argon2), so on a small server leave room for that.
- **Timeouts and worker recycling:** edit `gunicorn.conf.py`.
- **Where it listens:** in Docker, on port 8000 of the container (set by the
  Dockerfile), reached only by Caddy. Run directly on a Linux server, only on
  127.0.0.1:8000, so a reverse proxy on the same machine (e.g. Caddy or nginx) must
  forward requests to it. Start it from the project folder (or pass
  `-c gunicorn.conf.py`), or it silently ignores its settings file.

Check it: <http://localhost:8000/admin/> should give the site's "Page not found" page,
with no debug details. To see production refuse bad config without Docker:

```bash
DJANGO_READ_DOT_ENV=false uv run python manage.py check                 # error: DJANGO_SECRET_KEY missing
DJANGO_ENV=prod uv run python manage.py check                           # error: misspelled DJANGO_ENV
DJANGO_ENV=production DJANGO_DEBUG=true uv run python manage.py check   # error: DJANGO_DEBUG
```

### Uploaded files

Files people upload (a `FileField` or `ImageField`) are kept on this machine's disk by
default: `media/` in development, the `media` Docker volume in production. Development
serves them at `/media/` (only while debug is on, which is development's default).
Production never serves them from Django: Caddy serves them from the `media` volume at
`/media/`, always as downloads (`Content-Disposition: attachment`, `nosniff` and a
sandboxing Content Security Policy), so an uploaded HTML or SVG file can't run scripts
on the site. An upload never replaces an existing file with the same name; it gets a
new name.

To keep them in S3 or any S3-compatible service (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO,
...), set these in the app's environment (`.env.production` with Docker Compose):

| Variable | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `DJANGO_MEDIA_STORAGE` | `s3` (the default is `filesystem`) |
| `DJANGO_MEDIA_S3_BUCKET` | The bucket (required) |
| `DJANGO_MEDIA_S3_REGION` | The bucket's region, e.g. `eu-west-1`. Set it on AWS; other services say in their docs whether they need one (R2 uses `auto`) |
| `DJANGO_MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT_URL` | The service's address, for anything but AWS. Must be `https://` in production |
| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | A key for this bucket only. Leave both out on AWS to use the server's own role, which is safer than a long-lived key. They keep AWS's standard names (no `DJANGO_` prefix) because boto3 reads them itself |

The key (or the server's role) needs `s3:GetObject`, `s3:PutObject` and
`s3:DeleteObject` on `arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET/media/*`, and `s3:ListBucket` on
`arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET`. Without `ListBucket`, S3 answers "forbidden" instead of "not
found" when the starter checks a name is free, and uploads fail.

Files go under `media/` in the bucket and are private: nobody can open one without a
signed link. A field's `.url` (e.g. `document.attachment.url`) gives a link that stops
working after an hour, so call `.url` each time you show the file; don't save the link,
email it or cache a page holding it for longer than that. Opening a file downloads it
rather than showing it in the browser, so an uploaded HTML or SVG file can't run
scripts (images in `<img>` tags still show). Keep the bucket itself private (block
public access), and never serve it from the site's own domain.

Two uploads with the same name at the same moment can still both pass S3's "is this
name free" check, and one replaces the other. When a project adds uploads, give each
one its own folder in `upload_to` (e.g. a random UUID) so names never collide.

The settings refuse to start with an unknown `DJANGO_MEDIA_STORAGE`, an empty or missing
bucket, an address that isn't a full URL (or isn't `https://` in production), or only
one of the two keys.

### Email

`EMAIL_URL` sets how email is sent:

| Environment | Without `EMAIL_URL` | With it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Development | Emails are printed in the `runserver` terminal | Any form below, or `smtp://localhost:1025` (no login) for a local mail catcher |
| Tests | Emails are kept in `django.core.mail.outbox` | Ignored: tests never send real email, even if `.env` sets it |
| Production | The app runs, but sending email fails with "Email isn't set up" | `smtp+tls://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:587` (STARTTLS) or `smtp+ssl://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:465`, plus `DJANGO_DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL` |

The forms are `smtp+tls://`, `smtp+ssl://`, `smtp://` (no login, development only) and
`consolemail://` (print); anything else is refused in every environment, `smtps://`
included (it's unclear which kind of encryption it means). Production only takes
`smtp+tls://` or `smtp+ssl://`: plain `smtp://` would send the login and every email,
password reset links included, readable on the network.

Most mail services (Amazon SES, Postmark, Mailgun, ...) give you SMTP login details for
this, and only send From: addresses on a domain you've verified with them.
`DJANGO_DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL` is also the From: of Django's own messages
(`SERVER_EMAIL`). The connection gives up after 10 seconds, so a slow mail server can't
hold a request forever.

Percent-encode every character in the user or password other than letters, digits and
`- . _ ~` (for example `@` is `%40`, `/` is `%2F`, `#` is `%23`). Generated passwords
(Amazon SES's, for one) often contain `/` or `+`. This prints the encoded form:

```bash
python3 -c "import sys, urllib.parse; print(urllib.parse.quote(sys.argv[1], safe=''))" 'PASSWORD'
```

A password that wasn't encoded makes production refuse to start with "EMAIL_URL couldn't
be read", without showing the password.

A mail catcher such as Mailpit isn't included: if you run one, it takes mail on port
1025 and shows it in the browser (Mailpit at <http://localhost:8025>).

Check where email goes:

```bash
uv run python manage.py sendtestemail you@example.com     # development
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml exec web python manage.py sendtestemail you@example.com   # production
```

Without `EMAIL_URL`, the production one fails with "Email isn't set up".

### Background jobs

Slow work (sending email, resizing an upload, calling another service) can run outside
the request, with Django's Tasks API. Write a job as a function with `@task` in an
app's `tasks.py`, and enqueue it:

```python
# reports/tasks.py
from django.tasks import task


@task
def build_report(report_id):
    """Build the report and save it."""
```

```python
build_report.enqueue(report.id)  # in a view: returns at once, the job runs later
```

Arguments and return values must be JSON-friendly: pass an id, not a model instance,
and look the rest up inside the job. Never pass or return passwords, tokens, signed
links, API keys or personal data: in production they're stored in the database, with
the error of a failed job, and admins can read them.

Enqueue from inside a database transaction (a view saving something) with
`transaction.on_commit(lambda: build_report.enqueue(report.id))`, so the job only exists
if the save went through. In production a rolled-back job is never stored, but in
development and tests it would already have run.

| Environment | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| Development and tests | The job runs straight away, inside the request or test that enqueued it, so there's no worker to start. A job that raises an error doesn't break the request or fail the test: the result's `status` is `FAILED` and `result.errors` holds the error, so tests should check `result.status` |
| Production | The job is stored in the database (`django-tasks-db`), and the `worker` service (`manage.py db_worker`) runs it |

A few things only work in production, because development and tests store nothing:
running a job later (`build_report.using(run_after=...)`) and looking a result up by id
(`build_report.get_result(id)`). And there, arguments and results go through JSON, so
a dict's number keys arrive as strings and bytes can't be returned.

Jobs are kept in Postgres, not Redis, because Redis here is only a cache that saves
nothing to disk: jobs waiting in it would be lost on a restart. `django-tasks-db` is
pinned to one exact version because it's not yet 1.0, so a new version could change
its tables or worker. To update it, read its release notes, then run
`uv add django-tasks-db==<version>`, `uv run python manage.py migrate` and
`uv run pytest`.

The worker runs one job at a time. For more at once, start more copies, and they share
the work: add `--scale worker=2` to every `up` command (without it, the next deploy goes
back to one), e.g.
`docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml up --build -d --wait --scale worker=2`.

When the worker is stopped (a redeploy, or `down`), it finishes the job in progress
first, for up to 60 seconds (`stop_grace_period` in `compose.prod.yaml`). A job still
running after that, or when the server loses power, is killed and stays marked as
running for good: keep each job well under 60 seconds (split long work into smaller
jobs), and after an unclean stop, look in the admin's job list for jobs still running
and enqueue them again. Jobs waiting when you redeploy are run by the new code, so when
you change a job's arguments, keep accepting the old ones until those jobs have run (or
give the changed job a new name). Give network calls in jobs their own timeouts: a job
stuck on one holds up every job behind it. The worker's health check (see
[Production](#production-the-real-image-run-locally)) marks it unhealthy once jobs have
waited 5 minutes with none started, which Docker notices within about 3 more minutes.

In production, jobs and their results (including a failed job's error) are listed,
read-only, in the admin. Development and tests store nothing, so the list stays empty
there.

Finished jobs (successful or failed) stay in the database until you delete them. This
deletes those that finished more than 14 days ago (`--min-age-days 30` keeps a month):

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml run --rm worker python manage.py prune_db_task_results
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml run --rm worker python manage.py prune_db_task_results --dry-run   # only count them
```

Run it daily on the server, for example from cron. Cron has no terminal, so add `-T`,
and `cd` into the project folder first:

```bash
0 3 * * * cd /path/to/project && docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml run --rm -T worker python manage.py prune_db_task_results
```

### Error pages

These errors show the site's own pages from `templates/`, with no internal details:

| Page | When |
| --- | --- |
| `400.html` | A request the site can't use, e.g. a host name not in `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS` |
| `403.html` | Access denied (`PermissionDenied`) |
| `403_csrf.html` | A form failed its security check (the page was open too long, cookies are blocked, or it came from another site). With debug on it also shows Django's reason, e.g. "CSRF cookie not set" (`config/csrf_failure.py`) |
| `404.html` | No page at that address |
| `500.html` | The site crashed. The error is logged with its stack trace (see [Logs](#logs)) |

Other statuses (such as 405, a wrong HTTP method) keep Django's plain responses. In
development (debug on), Django shows its detailed debug pages for 400, 404 and 500
instead; the 403 pages show either way. The pages link to `/`, so give your project a
view at `/` (until then, the link shows the 404 page).

They extend `templates/base.html`, which a project's own pages can extend too: it's
plain HTML with the browser's own styles, in light or dark to match the visitor. Keep
`500.html` **and `base.html`** free of anything that needs the request, the user or the
database: `500.html` extends `base.html`, and Django shows it when those may be what
failed. For a nav bar or user menu, add a page that extends `base.html`, and have the
rest of your pages extend that.

To see the 400, 404 and 500 pages in development:

1. Add `DJANGO_DEBUG=false` to `.env`, and restart `runserver`.
2. Open <http://localhost:8000/no-such-page/>. (With debug off, `runserver` doesn't serve
   static files, so the admin looks unstyled.)
3. Remove the line and restart `runserver` when you're done.

`templates/proxy_error.html` is for the reverse proxy, not Django: a complete page,
loading nothing, that Caddy shows when Django doesn't answer (502, 503, 504: during a
restart, or when it's down), keeping the status (`handle_errors` in
`deploy/Caddyfile`). With nginx instead, the same is
`error_page 502 503 504 /proxy_error.html;` and
`location = /proxy_error.html { root /path/to/project/templates; internal; }`.

### Logs

Everything is written to the console, where `runserver`, Docker, systemd or the hosting
platform collects it. Development and tests write readable lines:

```text
2026-11-14 05:16:03,942 WARNING csp: CSP violation: ...
```

Production writes one JSON object per line, so log tools can search by field:

```json
{"time": "2026-11-14T05:16:03.942+00:00", "level": "ERROR", "logger": "django.request", "message": "Internal Server Error: /page/", "request": "GET /page/", "status": 500, "exception": "Traceback ..."}
```

- **Errors** always include their stack trace (`exception`), on the same line. They're
  never emailed, even if you set `ADMINS`: watch the logs, or send them to an error
  tracker. (To get emails, add Django's `mail_admins` handler to the `django` logger in
  `config/settings/logs.py`.) Python's warnings are logged too, as `py.warnings`.
- **Gunicorn's** own lines (`gunicorn.error`) and one line per request
  (`gunicorn.access`) are JSON too, with the request's details as text in `message`:
  address, method, path, status, bytes, milliseconds and browser. Behind the proxy
  (`DJANGO_BEHIND_PROXY`), the address is the visitor's, from the `X-Forwarded-For`
  that Caddy sets. Docker's health check adds a `GET /healthz` line every 30 seconds
  (from `127.0.0.1`), and a load balancer polling `/readyz` one line per poll. Caddy
  logs its own errors (certificates, a bad config) as JSON too, but not each request:
  the app's lines cover those.
- **What's in them:** request lines leave out query strings, which often carry tokens,
  but a path can still hold one (Django's password reset links do), the secret admin
  path shows up, and addresses and browsers are personal data. Keep logs as private as
  the database, and decide how long to keep them.
- **Less or more detail:** `DJANGO_LOG_LEVEL` is `INFO` by default. Set it to `WARNING`
  for less, or `DEBUG` for more (for a short while only: some libraries log request
  headers at `DEBUG`), in `.env` (development) or `.env.production` (the app and the
  worker), then restart with `docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml up -d`.
  Gunicorn's own lines stay at `INFO`; its `--log-level` option has no effect, because
  the levels are set in `gunicorn.conf.py`.
- **Not JSON:** command output (such as the `migrate` step's progress) is printed, not
  logged, and `runserver`'s request lines keep Django's own format.
- **Disk space:** every production container keeps up to 50 MB of log lines: 5 files of
  10 MB, all but the newest compressed, so they use far less disk (set in `x-logging`
  in `compose.prod.yaml`). Without a limit, Docker would keep them until the disk
  filled up. Older lines are dropped, and a container's logs are deleted when it's
  recreated (every deploy) or removed (`down`), so
  `docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs` only goes back to the last
  deploy, and less on a busy site. Anyone can push older lines out by sending enough
  requests, so to keep logs as evidence (or longer, or searchable), send them off the
  server to a log service. The files are Docker's standard `json-file` format, which
  log-shipping tools read; to use another log driver, replace the whole `x-logging`
  block.
- The formats are in `config/logging.py` and the setup in `config/settings/logs.py`;
  Gunicorn's is `logconfig_dict` in `gunicorn.conf.py`.

```bash
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs -f web worker   # follow production's logs
```

### Checking what's running, and cleaning up

```bash
docker compose ps                     # list development's running containers
docker compose down                   # stop and remove development's containers (data is kept)
docker compose down -v                # also DELETE development's database: all data is lost
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml ps        # the same for production
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml logs      # production's logs
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml down -v   # DELETES production's database and uploads
```

## Security

Passwords and login rate limiting are set in `config/settings/users.py`; HTTPS, HSTS, the
headers and the CSP in `config/settings/security.py`. They apply to every environment
unless noted.

- **Passwords** are hashed with Argon2 (tests use a fast hasher instead). Passwords saved
  with an older hasher still work, and are re-hashed with Argon2 the next time that user
  logs in. Each login check uses about 100 MB of memory for a moment, which matters
  when choosing `WEB_CONCURRENCY` on a small server.
- **Login rate limiting** ([django-axes](https://django-axes.readthedocs.io/) plus
  `config/login_limits.py`), with two limits:
  - 5 failed logins for the same username from the same address lock that pair out.
    Someone elsewhere can't lock a real user out this way.
  - 30 failed logins from one address, whatever usernames were tried, lock that address
    out. That stops anyone trying a new username each time (each try costs a full
    Argon2 check), and is high enough for people sharing an office address.

  A lockout lasts 15 minutes, and each further try during it restarts the full time.
  The visitor gets a "too many attempts" page with a `Retry-After` header. A
  successful login clears its pair's count. Change the limits with
  `DJANGO_LOGIN_FAILURE_LIMIT`, `DJANGO_LOGIN_IP_FAILURE_LIMIT` and
  `DJANGO_LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES` (each at least 1).

  Failed attempts are kept in the database (not Redis, which can be emptied) until their
  lockout time has passed, and listed in the admin under Axes, Access attempts. They hold
  the username as typed and the address; successful logins aren't recorded. Log lines
  hide both. IPv6 addresses count per /64, since one user usually controls a whole /64.

  Django's own login views (the admin, `LoginView`) are covered. Custom code must call
  `authenticate(request, username=..., password=...)` with the request, or the login is
  refused, and tests should log in with `client.force_login(user)` rather than
  `client.login(...)`. Behind a reverse proxy, `DJANGO_BEHIND_PROXY` must be true
  (`compose.prod.yaml` sets it, since Caddy is always in front; see
  [Production](#production-the-real-image-run-locally)); without it every visitor shares
  the proxy's address. To unlock early, including yourself in development
  (`<username>` is what was typed in the login form; you can also delete the entry in
  the admin):

  ```bash
  uv run python manage.py axes_reset                              # everyone
  uv run python manage.py axes_reset_username <username>          # one username
  uv run python manage.py axes_reset_ip <address>                 # one address
  docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.prod.yaml exec web python manage.py axes_reset   # in production
  ```
- **Security headers** on every response except `/healthz` and `/readyz`: `X-Frame-Options: DENY`,
  `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy: same-origin` and
  `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin`. Production adds the HTTPS redirect,
  HTTPS-only cookies and HSTS (one year, subdomains included; on a brand-new domain
  start with `DJANGO_SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS=3600`). Each can be changed with its
  `DJANGO_SECURE_*` variable in `.env.example`.
- **Content Security Policy** (`SECURE_CSP`, Django's built-in one): browsers load only
  what the policy lists and block everything else, so code injected into a page doesn't
  run. Scripts, styles, images, fonts and requests come from this site only; anything
  not listed (frames, audio and video, workers) is blocked until a project allows it.
  - Inline code needs the page's nonce: `<script nonce="{{ '{{' }} csp_nonce {{ '}}' }}">` or
    `<style nonce="{{ '{{' }} csp_nonce {{ '}}' }}">`.
  - Never use `style="..."` attributes, `onclick=`-style handlers or `javascript:` links:
    put them in a static CSS or JS file.
  - A view that needs something from another site (a map, a video) can set its own
    policy with `csp_override` from `django.views.decorators.csp`. It **replaces** the
    whole policy for that view, so build on the site's one rather than starting empty:
    `@csp_override({**settings.SECURE_CSP, "frame-src": ["https://www.youtube-nocookie.com"]})`.
    Never pass `{}`: that removes the policy for the view.
  - In **development** the policy only reports (Django's yellow debug pages use inline
    styles): the browser's console shows what it would block. Tests and production
    enforce it, so check the console before you commit.
  - Browsers send what the policy blocked to `/csp-report/` (the policy's `report-uri`),
    which logs a short summary as a `csp` warning: the page, the rule and what was
    blocked, without logins, query strings or code samples. A run of them after a
    deploy usually means the policy blocks something a page needs; a few odd ones are
    often browser extensions. In development they also show in the `runserver`
    terminal, including expected ones from Django's yellow error pages. Reports bigger
    than 10 KB, or not sent as `application/csp-report`, are refused. Anyone can send
    reports, so a flood shows up in the logs until the proxy's rate limits (a coming
    version of the starter) slow it.
- **HSTS preload is off on purpose.** Being on browsers' preload list is hard to undo
  (removal takes months) and covers every subdomain, so each project opts in when it's
  ready:
  1. Make sure HTTPS works on the domain and every subdomain.
  2. Keep `DJANGO_SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS` at 31536000 or more and
     `DJANGO_SECURE_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS` true.
  3. In `config/settings/security.py`, set `SECURE_HSTS_PRELOAD = True` and delete the
     `SILENCED_SYSTEM_CHECKS` line.
  4. In `tests/test_security_headers.py`, change
     `test_production_sends_hsts_for_a_year_without_preloading` to expect preloading.
  5. Deploy, then submit the domain at <https://hstspreload.org>.

## Checks

### On every commit (pre-commit)

`.pre-commit-config.yaml` runs these each time you commit, and blocks the commit if
one fails:

- **gitleaks**: finds passwords, keys and tokens in the changes being committed.
- **ruff check** and **ruff format --check**: lint and formatting, with the
  project's own ruff.

Set it up once in each clone, after `uv sync` (the project must be a git repo):

```bash
uv run pre-commit install          # runs the checks on every commit from now on
uv run pre-commit run --all-files  # runs ruff on every file, any time
```

The first commit after `pre-commit install` is slow and needs the internet:
pre-commit downloads and builds gitleaks once, then reuses it.

gitleaks only looks at what's staged for a commit, so under `--all-files` it only
scans whatever is currently staged (usually nothing): it isn't a full scan. To scan **every file** or the **whole git history**, use
gitleaks' official Docker image (the same version as the hook, pinned to its exact
image, with the project mounted read-only):

```bash
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:v8.30.1@sha256:c00b6bd0aeb3071cbcb79009cb16a60dd9e0a7c60e2be9ab65d25e6bc8abbb7f dir /repo --redact   # every file
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:v8.30.1@sha256:c00b6bd0aeb3071cbcb79009cb16a60dd9e0a7c60e2be9ab65d25e6bc8abbb7f git /repo --redact   # the whole history
```

The **every file** scan also reads files git ignores, such as your local `.env` and
`.venv/`. A finding there isn't a committed secret (git ignores `.env` files, so they
aren't committed unless forced); the **whole history** scan shows what was actually
committed.

If gitleaks blocks a commit, remove the secret instead of skipping the check
(never `git commit --no-verify`). If the secret was ever pushed, treat it as leaked
and replace it.

If a ruff check fails with "files were modified by this hook" and `uv.lock` has
changed, `pyproject.toml` was changed without updating the lock file. Stage
`uv.lock` and commit again.

**Updating gitleaks:** the hook is pinned to a commit, not a tag, because tags can
be moved. Run `uv run pre-commit autoupdate --freeze` and check the version in its
`# frozen:` comment. Then update the Docker commands above to the same version and
its digest (`docker pull ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:<version>`, then
`docker inspect ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:<version> --format '{% raw %}{{index .RepoDigests 0}}{% endraw %}'`).

### Before every pull request

```bash
uv run pytest --cov
(f=$(mktemp) && trap 'rm -f "$f"' EXIT && uv export --locked --format requirements-txt --no-emit-project --quiet -o "$f" && uv run pip-audit --disable-pip --require-hashes -r "$f")
```

- **Tests and coverage:** `uv run pytest --cov` runs every test (Postgres must be
  running) and **fails if total coverage drops below 80%**. Coverage is set up in
  `pyproject.toml` (`[tool.coverage.*]`): it measures the project's code (not the
  migrations or the tests), checks both sides of every `if`, and lists the lines no
  test runs. For a browsable report as well, run
  `uv run pytest --cov --cov-report=term-missing --cov-report=html` (it writes
  `htmlcov/`; naming `--cov-report` replaces the default list, so ask for both).
  `tests/test_migrations.py` also fails if a model changed without a migration.
  Any use of a Django feature that's being removed (in the next version or the one
  after) fails the tests too, wherever it happens: in the code the tests run and in
  the `manage.py` processes some tests start, so an upgrade never brings surprises.
  (One gap: code passed to `manage.py shell -c` counts as Django's own, so Django
  doesn't warn about it.) Fix the code as the warning's message says. If the warning
  comes from an installed package rather than your own code, the traceback shows the
  package's path: update it (`uv lock --upgrade-package <name>`, then `uv sync`), and
  if no fixed version exists yet, decide with your team before changing the rules
  (`filterwarnings` in `pyproject.toml`).
- **pip-audit** checks every package in `uv.lock` (exact versions and hashes,
  including the dev tools) against the public vulnerability databases. It prints
  "No known vulnerabilities found", or each problem with the version that fixes it;
  the usual fix is `uv lock --upgrade-package <name>`. The command writes the locked
  packages to a temporary file first, so if `uv.lock` is out of date with
  `pyproject.toml` the whole command fails instead of checking nothing.

