| `config/urls.py` | The root URL map; each app adds its URLs here. Also `/csp-report/`, and uploaded files at `/media/` in development |
| `config/middleware.py` | Answers `/healthz` and `/readyz` before anything else runs |
| `config/health.py` | The checks: the database and Redis answer (`/readyz`), and the worker keeps up (`python -m config.health worker`) |
| `config/logging.py` | The log formats: one JSON object per line in production, readable text otherwise |
| `config/csp_report.py` | Logs a short summary of each report of what the Content Security Policy blocked (`/csp-report/`) |
| `config/csrf_failure.py` | The page for a form that failed its security check, with Django's reason when debug is on |
| `config/email_not_set_up.py` | The email backend production uses without `EMAIL_URL`: sending fails with a clear error instead of the email being lost |
| `config/login_limits.py` | Login rate limiting's own parts: the visitor's address (a proxy's header only when `DJANGO_BEHIND_PROXY` is on, IPv6 per /64) and the per-address limit |
| `config/wsgi.py`, `config/asgi.py` | Entry points the production server starts from |
| `accounts/` | The custom `User` model: unique lowercase email, `public_id` UUID |
| `templates/` | Project-wide templates: `base.html`, the error pages (`400`, `403`, `403_csrf`, `404`, `500`) and `proxy_error.html` for the reverse proxy |
| `tests/test_healthz.py` | Proves `/healthz` answers any host without the database |
| `tests/test_error_pages.py` | Proves each error (400, 403, failed form check, 404, 500) shows the site's page without internal details, a failed form check gives its reason only with debug on, a crash shows the 500 page with the database and Redis down, pages always have a title, no page has code the CSP would block, and the proxy's page loads nothing |
| `tests/test_readyz.py` | Proves `/readyz` is `ok` only when the database and Redis answer, a quick 503 when the database refuses or never answers (while `/healthz` still answers), the database check uses its own connection, failures are logged without connection details, and the worker's check fails when the database is down or jobs wait with none started, but not for a busy worker, finished jobs, other queues or Redis being down |
| `tests/test_production_settings.py` | Proves production is secure and refuses bad config |
| `tests/test_migrations.py` | Fails if a model changed without a migration |
| `tests/test_deprecation_warnings.py` | Proves every Django deprecation warning (a deprecated setting included) fails the tests, both in pytest itself and in the `manage.py` processes tests start, that those processes otherwise run as `python manage.py` would, and that other libraries' deprecations don't fail the tests |
| `tests/test_settings_package.py` | Proves every topic file is combined and its settings reach Django, no two topic files set the same setting differently, and a leftover `config/settings.py` is refused |
| `tests/test_env_example.py` | Fails if `.env.example` misses a variable read in the project's Python files (except tests, migrations and virtualenvs) or compose files, lists one nothing reads or lists one twice, has a line that isn't a plain `NAME=value` (indented, or with spaces, quotes or a comment in the value), or shows a wrong `DJANGO_ENV` or true/false value |
| `tests/test_ignore_files.py` | Fails if `.gitignore` lets a `.env` file or private key through (`.env`, `.env.local`, `config/.env`, `*.pem`, `id_rsa`, ...) or leaves out `.env.example`, or if `.dockerignore` lets any of them into the image (both checked with git's matching, plus a check that Docker's rules cover every folder) |
| `tests/test_compose.py` | Proves development stays on this machine, and production takes its secrets only from the env files, migrates before the app and the worker start, runs locked down, publishes only Caddy (with the app always behind it), keeps the database and Redis internal, keeps Redis a cache only (a login, nothing on disk, a memory limit), limits every service's logs, gives Gunicorn time to stop and the worker time to finish its job, and that the Caddyfile is valid |
| `tests/test_caddy.py` | Runs the real Caddy image with `deploy/Caddyfile` and proves the health checks are hidden, uploads are downloads that can't run scripts, and with the app down visitors get the site's page with the real status |
| `tests/test_security_headers.py` | Proves pages, error pages and static files send the security headers and exactly the strict CSP with a fresh nonce, the admin has nothing the CSP would block, development only reports it, passwords use Argon2 (older hashes still work and get upgraded), and production sends HSTS over HTTPS without preloading |
| `tests/test_login_rate_limit.py` | Proves repeated wrong passwords lock that username out from that address (even with the right password), a new username each time still hits the per-address limit, others aren't affected, lockouts end after `DJANGO_LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES`, a successful login clears the count, the limits come from the environment, and a faked `X-Forwarded-For` can't get around them |
| `tests/test_tasks.py` | Proves a job runs straight away in development and tests, production stores jobs in the database, and the worker runs a stored job and records a failing one as failed |
| `tests/test_email.py` | Proves email is printed in development, always kept in memory in tests, and in production either fails clearly when not set up or goes over an encrypted connection (with a From address and a timeout), a login never goes over plain SMTP, and unsafe, incomplete or unreadable `EMAIL_URL`s (such as a password that wasn't percent-encoded) are refused without showing the password |
| `tests/test_uploaded_files.py` | Proves uploads are local by default, never replace an existing file and can't escape the media folder, only development serves them, S3 files are private downloads with SigV4 links that expire after an hour, keys come from the environment and never appear in Django's settings (without them, the server's role is used), and bad upload settings are refused |
| `tests/test_logs.py` | Proves log lines are one JSON object each in production (stack traces and line breaks included, request query strings left out) and readable text in development, errors are logged once with their stack trace and never emailed, and Gunicorn can set up the same JSON logs without query strings |
| `tests/test_csp_report.py` | Proves a CSP report is logged as a short summary without query strings, samples or fake extra lines, and that huge reports, things that aren't reports and GETs are refused |
| `tests/test_gunicorn_config.py` | Proves `gunicorn.conf.py` uses only real Gunicorn settings with valid values |
| `Dockerfile` | The production image: uv, non-root user, Gunicorn |
| `gunicorn.conf.py` | Gunicorn's settings: workers, timeouts, worker recycling, JSON logs to stdout and stderr |
| `compose.yaml` | What every environment shares: Postgres 18 and Redis 8 (the cache), with their health checks |
| `compose.override.yaml` | Development, loaded automatically: the dev login and the Postgres and Redis ports |
| `compose.prod.yaml` | Production: Postgres, Redis, the `migrate` step, the app, the job worker and Caddy, with settings and secrets from `.env.production`, `.env.postgres`, `.env.redis` and `.env.caddy`, and at most 50 MB of logs per container |
| `compose.local-http.yaml` | Only for running production on your machine over plain HTTP: switches the HTTPS settings off and has Caddy answer on `localhost:8000` without a certificate. Never on a server |
| `deploy/Caddyfile` | Caddy's settings: HTTPS, forwarding to the app, uploads as downloads, the proxy error page, hiding the health checks |
| `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock` | Dependencies and tool settings (pytest, Ruff, coverage) |
| `.pre-commit-config.yaml` | The checks run on every commit: gitleaks, ruff |
| `.env.example` | Every environment variable, documented |
| `.gitignore`, `.dockerignore` | Files kept out of git and out of the image. Every file whose name starts with `.env` (including `.envrc`), in any folder, is kept out of both; git keeps only `.env.example`. Private keys, certificates, keystores and SSH keys (`*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `*.jks`, `id_rsa` and the like) are kept out of both too. To commit another such file, add a `!` line for it to `.gitignore` |
| `.copier-answers.yml` | Lets `copier update` find the template; don't edit |

## Conventions

- Project-wide templates (`base.html`, the error pages) live in the root `templates/`;
  each app keeps its own in `<app>/templates/<app>/`.
- Models expose `public_id` (a UUID) in URLs and APIs, never the database `id`.
- Business logic lives in plain functions (`services.py`, `selectors.py`) that views and APIs share.
